        "thinking": "So the `Container` type in CF Workers is NOT the same as what I was thinking. The `Container` interface is exposed as `this.ctx.container` (via the Durable Object context), where the DO itself manages a container. This means:\n\n1. You create a Durable Object class\n2. The DO instance manages a container lifecycle via `this.ctx.container`\n3. The container's ports are accessed via `this.ctx.container.getTcpPort(port)` which returns a `Fetcher`\n\nSo the architecture for CF Containers is:\n\n\n- Define a DO class that wraps the container, declare it in `wrangler.toml` under `[[durable_objects.bindings]]`, and specify the container image in `[[containers]]` with `class_name` pointing to that DO\n- The DO class then has access to `this.ctx.container` to start and monitor the container, plus get Fetchers to communicate with its ports\n- I'm sketching out a `SidecarContainer` class that checks if the container is running on each request and starts it if needed\n\nWriting container logic... I see the issue now — the `[[containers]]` section needs a `class_name` that references a Durable Object, and the actual binding goes in `[[durable_objects.bindings]]`, not inside containers. So I need to create wrapper Durable Object classes for both the sidecar and plugin containers, set up the bindings correctly in wrangler.toml, and then update the Worker code to instantiate and call them properly.\n\nActually, I realize DurableObject stubs have `fetch()` methods, so I can use the namespace pattern to get a stub and call fetch on it directly — similar to what I already have but using `DurableObjectNamespace` instead of `Fetcher`. The tricky part is that containers won't run locally in wrangler dev since Docker isn't available, so I need the fallback mechanism to handle that gracefully.\n\nFor local development, the `SIDECAR_SERVICE` binding would point to a DO stub, but since the container isn't actually running, the fetch would fail and we'd need to fall back to KV. In production, the container would be running and the DO fetch would work. So the strategy is to keep the container config commented out or optional, make local dev work with KV as the fallback, and document what's needed to enable containers in production. The workflow code currently types `SIDECAR_SERVICE` as `Fetcher?`, but if I change it to `DurableObjectNamespace`, I need to make sure the rest of the code aligns with that type.\n\nI'm weighing three approaches: creating a proper DurableObject that proxies to the container and handles the local dev case with a stub or fallback, simply removing the broken container sections and keeping the optional Fetcher pattern with KV fallback, or fixing the wrangler.toml configuration directly.\n\nGiven that local dev is currently broken, I'm going to take a pragmatic hybrid approach: immediately fix wrangler.toml by removing the problematic binding, create proper DurableObject wrapper classes for the container API, add commented-out configuration showing how to enable containers in production, and update the environment types and workflows to use the DurableObjectNamespace pattern while keeping the KV fallback for when containers aren't available.\n\nNow I'm realizing the core issue — wrangler dev doesn't actually support running Docker containers locally, so even with valid wrangler.toml configuration, local development won't work with containers enabled.\n\nThe validation error is preventing the dev server from starting at all, but fixing the config format should let it start even if the container bindings aren't functional. The real solution is to make the fallback logic handle cases where `this.env.SIDECAR_SERVICE` is either undefined or throws errors when called, so the workflow can gracefully degrade to using KV storage instead.\n\nFixing the wrangler.toml configuration... Writing container dispatch logic... I need to verify whether `this.ctx` in `DurableObjectEnv` actually has access to the `container` property, and I'm checking the workers-types to confirm it's available. There's a timing issue though — when the container isn't running, calling `start()` doesn't guarantee immediate readiness, so I need to look at the Container interface to see if there's a way to wait for it to be ready, like using `monitor()` to track its state.\n\nSince `start()` is fire-and-forget and `monitor()` only tells us when the container exits, I'll implement a retry pattern with backoff to handle the startup delay. Given the complexity of properly implementing Durable Object container classes and potential limitations with local wrangler development, I'm going to focus on the most pragmatic solution that unblocks the user right now.",
